course: euphemisms
lesson: 41
title: Olympiad: the euphemism detective ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In an olympiad puzzle about soft words you are a detective: first turn every soft phrase into plain words, then use the plain meanings for the next step of the puzzle.
explain: Many puzzles are odd-one-out: three phrases share one plain meaning and the fourth does not, and you must name it and say why.
explain: Watch for traps: the same word can mean different things ("bit the dust" is a death for a villain but a breakdown for a car, "lost" can mean died, went mad or mislaid), and the scene tells you which.
explain: Some puzzles count: how many people in a story are still alive, how many really drank, and only the plain meanings are counted, never a hyperbole like "a million times".
explain: Picture Gran saying "Uncle Bob's popped his clogs, Auntie May's no spring chicken": Bob has died, but May is only old, so she still counts among the living.
fact: To solve an odd-one-out, first turn each phrase into plain words, then look for the one whose plain meaning is different.
fact: "Popped his clogs", "snuffed it", "brown bread" and "cashed in his chips" all mean died; they are joking or slangy.
fact: "Getting on", "no spring chicken" and "past his prime" mean old, not dead.
fact: "Laid up" means ill or injured in bed, not dead; "laid to rest" means buried.
fact: "Put to sleep" means an animal was killed gently by a vet; "crossed the rainbow bridge" means a pet died.
fact: "Fixed" (US) and "seen to" (UK) mean a pet had an operation so it cannot have babies; "rehomed" means given to new owners.
fact: "Bit the dust" can mean died, or broke for good when it is said about a machine.
fact: "Spend a penny", "powder my nose" and "freshen up" can all mean go to the toilet.
fact: "Let go", "shown the door", "laid off" and "relieved of his duties" all mean lost a job; "relieved of his duties" is official, "shown the door" is everyday talk.
fact: "Telling porkies", "economical with the truth" and "bending the truth" mean lying; "pulling your leg" means fooling you as a friendly joke.
fact: "Drat", "blast", "sugar", "fudge" and "darn" are mild stand-ins for swear words.
fact: "Gosh", "gee", "jeepers", "cripes" and "for Pete's sake" are mild stand-ins for holy names: God, Jesus and Christ.
fact: "Merry" and "squiffy" mean a little drunk; "blotto" and "three sheets to the wind" mean very drunk; "had a few" means has drunk enough to be drunk.
fact: "A bit dim", "not the sharpest tool in the shed" and "no Einstein" mean not clever; "off one's rocker" and "round the twist" mean mad.
fact: "Inside", "up the river" and "guest of His Majesty" all mean in prison; "under the influence" means drunk or on drugs.
fact: Rhyming slang swaps a word for a phrase that rhymes with it: "brown bread" means dead because "bread" rhymes with "dead", and "porkies" comes from "porky pies", which rhymes with "lies".
fact: "The late" before a name means the person has died, so "the late Mr Jones" can never come to next week's party.
fact: "Lost her husband" means he died; "lost the plot" and "lost his marbles" mean went mad or got very confused.
fact: Hyperbole makes things sound bigger ("I've told you a million times"); understatement makes them sound smaller ("a bit of a pickle"); a euphemism makes a hard thing sound softer.
fact: A white lie is a small kind lie to spare feelings, such as praising a present you do not like.
exercise: Odd one out: "popped his clogs", "snuffed it", "no spring chicken", "cashed in his chips". | "No spring chicken": old, not dead | "Snuffed it": a candle went out | "Popped his clogs": took off shoes | "Cashed in his chips": won at cards
exercise: Gran: "Uncle Bob popped his clogs, Auntie May's no spring chicken, and Grandad's laid up with a tummy bug." How many of the three are still alive? | Two: May and Grandad | One: only Auntie May | None of the three | All three of them
exercise: Odd one out: "drat", "sugar", "gosh", "fudge". | "Gosh": it hides a holy name | "Drat": it is a kind of sweet | "Sugar": it is not a stand-in | "Fudge": it hides the word "God"
exercise: Kit says "squiffy" is worse than "blotto". Is he right? | No: squiffy is a little drunk, blotto very drunk | Yes: squiffy is very drunk, blotto a little | No: neither word has anything to do with drink | Yes: both mean drunk, but squiffy is much the ruder word
exercise: Dad: "The old telly finally bit the dust, and so did Mr Grimes's goldfish." What happened? | The telly broke and the goldfish died | Both the telly and the fish died | The telly got dusty and the fish died | The fish broke the telly
exercise: Which sentence uses "lost" to mean that someone died? | "She lost her gran in May." | "She lost the plot in May." | "She lost her keys in May." | "She lost the match in May."
exercise: In rhyming slang "porky pies" means lies because "pies" rhymes with "lies". Using the same trick, what does "apples and pears" mean? | Stairs | A fruit bowl | Two best friends | A market stall
exercise: How many of these mean someone lost a job: "let go", "shown the door", "laid up", "laid off", "relieved of his duties"? | 4 | 5 | 3 | 2
exercise: Which person is saying something untrue only as a friendly joke? | Uncle, who is pulling your leg | The minister, economical with the truth | The boy who told the police porkies | The seller who was bending the truth
exercise: Odd one out: "inside", "up the river", "guest of His Majesty", "under the influence". | "Under the influence": drunk, not in prison | "Inside": indoors, not in prison | "Up the river": in a boat | "Guest of His Majesty": staying at a royal palace
exercise: A vet's note says "Max has been fixed." Max is a dog. Which is true? | Max had an operation so he cannot have puppies | Max was broken and has now been mended by the vet | Max was put to sleep | Max has learned to sit and stay
exercise: Odd one out: "gee", "cripes", "jeepers", "blast". | "Blast": it stands in for "damn" | "Gee": it stands in for "damn" | "Cripes": it is a real swear | "Jeepers": it means "goodness me"
exercise: Which line is understatement, not hyperbole? | "It's a bit nippy," in a blizzard | "I've told you a million times!" | "This bag weighs a ton." | "I'm literally starving to death right now."
exercise: A poster says: "The late Mrs Ali will open the fete next Saturday." What is wrong with it? | Mrs Ali has died, so she cannot open it | Mrs Ali will arrive late to the fete | The fete is on the wrong day | It should say "the early Mrs Ali"
exercise: Mum: "Grandad's had a few, Aunt Jo's squiffy, Uncle Pete's on orange juice, and Nan's three sheets to the wind." How many have drunk alcohol? | Three | Four | Two | One
exercise: "The cat was seen to, the rabbit crossed the rainbow bridge and the hamster was rehomed." Which pet is no longer alive? | The rabbit | The cat, after its operation | The hamster, in its new home | All three of them
exercise: Which two phrases mean the same thing? | "Spend a penny" and "powder my nose" | "Spend a penny" and "cash-strapped" | "Laid up" and "laid to rest" | "Fixed" and "put to sleep"
exercise: Odd one out by who says it: "involuntary separation", "relieved of his duties", "reduction in force", "shown the door". | "Shown the door": everyday talk, not official | "Reduction in force": it is about the army | "Involuntary separation": a divorce | "Relieved of his duties": given a long holiday from work
exercise: Teacher: "Mr Hall, no sorry, the late Mr Hall, built this hall." Why did she correct herself? | She remembered that Mr Hall has died | She remembered he built it late at night | She got his name wrong at first | She remembered he is late today
exercise: Ella says "not the sharpest tool in the shed" and "off his rocker" mean the same. Is she right? | No: one means not clever, the other means mad | Yes: both of them mean that someone is mad | Yes: both of them mean not clever | No: one is about tools, the other one is about chairs
test: "bruv my nan says grandad's brown bread and the dog's been seen to. who's dead then" | Only Grandad; the dog was neutered | Grandad and the dog are both dead | Only the dog; Grandad is eating | Nobody; they are both just poorly
test: Our kid: "I've told you a million times, the cat's not dead, it's just been fixed!" How many times did he really say it, and what happened to the cat? | Many times; the cat was neutered | A million times; the cat was neutered | Many times; the cat was put to sleep | A million times; the cat was mended
test: "Dad's a stubby short of a six-pack and the ute's bitten the dust, heaps of drama this arvo lol" (a ute is a small truck). What is true? | The ute broke down; Dad is teased as silly | The ute got dusty on the road; Dad drank six beers | The ute broke down; Dad has died | The ute is brand new; Dad is short
test: Your mate fell off the shed roof: "it's just a scratch, I'm fine", but his arm is bent at a funny angle. What is wise? | He may be badly hurt: tell an adult now | Believe him, it is only a scratch | Laugh, he is pulling your leg | Wait till tomorrow and see how it is
test: Kayden reckons "fudge!" is only ever a sweet, so it can never be a swear. right? | No: shouted when cross, it stands in for the f-word | Yes: it is only ever the name of a soft brown sweet | No: "fudge" is the f-word itself | Yes: it only stands in for "God"
test: "Och, the wee dog was put to sleep at the vet's, the bairn's that upset." What happened to the dog? | The vet ended its life gently | The dog had a long nap | The dog had a small operation | The dog was given to a new family
test: News: "The minister, who had been economical with the truth, was relieved of his duties." What came first? | He lied | He was sacked | He went home to his family | He was given a new job
test: Odd one out in a school report: "sociable", "strong personality", "enthusiastic contributor", "always hands in neat homework". | "Always hands in neat homework": plain praise | "Sociable": it means quiet and shy | "Strong personality": real praise | "Enthusiastic contributor": calm and waits to be asked
test: Gran's "feeling delicate" after the wedding, Grandad's "a bit peckish", Uncle Ray's "worse for wear". Who drank too much? | Gran and Uncle Ray | Only Grandad | All three of them | Only Uncle Ray
test: Mum: "I've asked you a hundred times! Your 2 mates are no Einsteins, they left 3 cups on the floor." How many cups must be picked up? | 3 | 100 | 2 | 103
test: "y'all, the boss told my cousin 'we've decided to go in a different direction', he's fixin' to celebrate." Should he celebrate? | No: it means he was turned down | Yes: it means he got the job | Yes: it means a new office | No: it means he must drive elsewhere
test: Which one is a white lie, not a serious lie? | "Love the jumper, Nan!" about one you dislike | A minister "misremembering" a secret payment | A thief telling porkies to the police | Hiding the real cost of a new bridge
exam: Identify the expression that does not denote death: "snuffed it", "flatlined", "brown bread", "past his prime". | "Past his prime" | "Snuffed it" | "Flatlined" | "Brown bread"
exam: Given that "squiffy" denotes slight and "blotto" severe intoxication, determine the ordering that proceeds from least to most severe. | Sober, squiffy, blotto | Squiffy, sober, blotto | Blotto, squiffy, sober | Sober, blotto, squiffy
exam: Of the expressions "gosh", "cripes", "darn" and "jeepers", identify that which replaces a swear word rather than a holy name. | "Darn" | "Gosh" | "Cripes" | "Jeepers"
exam: Ascertain which of the following constitutes understatement rather than hyperbole. | Describing a deep cut as "a scratch" | Claiming to have waited "forever" | Describing a bag as weighing "a ton" | Claiming to have told someone "a million times"
